          "finish_reason": "length",
          "answer_excerpt": "# Technical Analysis of Plant Condition Records (Records 0000000\u20130000017)\n\n## 1. Scope and Method\n\nThis report analyzes 18 condition-monitoring records spanning eight asset classes (pumps, valves, separators, condensers, heat exchangers, pipelines, reactors, compressors, scrubbers, and turbines) across 17 stations. Each record contains five measured parameters: pressure (kPa), temperature (\u00b0C), flow (m\u00b3/h), vibration (mm/s RMS), and efficiency (%).\n\nThe analysis proceeds in three stages:\n\n1. **Screening against reference thresholds** \u2014 vibration per ISO 10816/20816 vibration severity zones, and process parameters against typical design envelopes for each asset class.\n2. **Failure-mode inference** \u2014 correlating parameter combinations with known degradation mechanisms.\n3. **Prioritized maintenance recommendations** \u2014 ranked by risk and urgency.\n\nA note on data quality: the records are sparse (five parameters, no trend history, no metadata on sensor location or units' operating point). Conclusions are therefore probabilistic \u2014 they identify *likely* failure modes and *priority* inspection targets, not confirmed diagnoses. Where a parameter is anomalous in isolation, I flag it as \"verify\" rather than \"act.\"\n\n",
